{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And they will say to me, What is His name?</b> This verse tells you its interpretation. For it is not fitting that Moses should say <b>And they will say to me, What is His name?</b> as a sign that they will believe in him, since asking His name and telling it to them is not a sign for someone who does not believe in Him in the first place — for if Israel knew that name, Moses too would have known it, and behold his knowledge is like their knowledge, and it is not a sign or wonder at all. And if they had never heard it, what proof would there be that they would believe his words at all? And behold, after He made known to him the great Name, He said, \"And if they do not believe me\" (Exodus 4:1), and only then did He give him signs.\n\nRabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 3:13) that Moses asked what name he should say to Israel from among His names, for with El Shaddai He does not perform signs, but only with the great Name. And his words are not correct in my eyes, for it had not yet been said to Moses that he would perform signs and great wonders at the Exodus from Egypt, but rather that He would save them from the hand of Egypt and bring them up to the land — and with the name El Shaddai it would be possible to come into Pharaoh's heart to save them and also to defeat the nations. And already He brought Sarah out of his [Pharaoh's] house with the great plagues that He brought upon him, and He granted Abraham victory alone over the great kings, and all this was with the help of El Shaddai, the name known to the Patriarchs — and so He would do for their children. Moreover, Jacob said, \"And may Elohim be with you and restore you to the land of your fathers\" (Genesis 48:21), and Joseph said, \"Elohim will surely remember you\" (Genesis 50:25), and behold the remembrance is attributed to Elohim.\n\nIn my opinion, Moses was at that time already a father of wisdom, great in the rank of prophecy, and by way of inquiry he asked that He inform him who the Sender is — that is, in which attribute He is sent to them — as in the matter that Isaiah said, \"And now Adonai Elohim has sent me, and His spirit\" (Isaiah 48:16). And behold, he said: they will ask me about my mission — whether it is in the attribute of El Shaddai, which stood for the Patriarchs, or in the attribute of supernal mercy, through which signs and wonders, newly created, will be performed. And this is because He had said to him, \"I am the Elohim of your father, the Elohim of Abraham\" (Exodus 3:6), but did not specify any of His holy names at all. And Moses understood that He was promising him the standing at Mount Sinai and the giving of the Torah, and he is one who knows that the Torah will not be given in the name El Shaddai mentioned in connection with the Patriarchs, but rather in the great Name by which the world was made. Therefore he asked, <b>What shall I say to them?</b>\n\nOur Sages raised this difficulty (Exodus Rabbah 3): they said, <b>And Moses said to Elohim, Behold I come to the children of Israel</b>. Rabbi Shimon said in the name of Rabbi Simon: Moses said, I am destined to act as an intermediary between You and them. When You give them the Torah and say to them, \"I am YHWH your Elohim — <b>the Elohim of your fathers has sent me to you</b>,\" at that moment Moses will be found out regarding his affairs — for he feared that if they ask him what His name is: <b>What shall I say to them?</b> — at that moment Moses was asking that HaShem inform him of the great Name. And this is the meaning of the question." "And behold, Elohim answered him, <b>I AM THAT I AM</b> — I will be with you in this trouble, and I will be with you in other troubles. He said before Him: Master of the universe, it is enough for trouble in its time. He said to him: You have spoken well; thus shall you say to the children of Israel, etc. This is the language of Rashi (on Exodus 3:14), from the words of our Sages (Berakhot 9a; Exodus Rabbah 3).\n\nAnd their intent in this is that Moses said before Him, blessed be He, <b>And they will say to me, What is His name?</b> — that He should tell them a name that indicates complete proof concerning existence and concerning providence. And HaShem answered him: Why should they ask for My name? They have no need for another proof, for I will be with them in every trouble — when they call upon Me I will answer them. And this is the great proof that there is Elohim in Israel, that they are near to us whenever we call upon Him, and that there is an Elohim who judges in the land. And this is a correct interpretation of this Aggadah.\n\nThey also said something similar in a midrash of Aggadah (Menorat ha-Ma'or, First Lamp, chapter on Charity): And what is <b>I AM THAT I AM</b>? Just as you are with Me, so am I with you — if they open their hands and give charity, I too will open My hand, as it is written, \"YHWH will open for you His good treasure\" (Deuteronomy 28:12). And if they do not open their hands, what is written there? \"Behold, He restrains the waters and they dry up\" (Job 12:15), etc.\n\nThey further interpreted: Rabbi Isaac said, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Say to them, I am He who was, and now I am He, and I am He for the time to come. Therefore <b>Ehyeh</b> is written here three times (Exodus Rabbah 3:6). And the explanation of Rabbi Isaac's opinion is that because past and future time are all in the Creator in the present — for there is no change or succession with Him, and none of His days have passed at all — therefore all times are called in Him by a single name, indicating the necessity of existence.\n\n<b>Onkelos</b> translated both names: \"I will be with whoever is.\" And he did not translate at all the name that He told him: <b>Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: Ehyeh has sent me to you.</b> What appears from his opinion is that in his view the revered name YHWH, which he made known to him, is these four letters that He commanded him: \"Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: Ehyeh has sent me to you.\" But He first informed him of its meaning, for Moses' question was to know the ways of HaShem from His name, as he later said again, \"Make known to me now Your ways, that I may know You\" (Exodus 33:13). And when He answered him there, \"And I will proclaim the name of YHWH before you, and I will be gracious to whom I will be gracious, and I will show mercy to whom I will show mercy\" (Exodus 33:19), saying that by this revered name which will be proclaimed before Him, He grants grace and mercy — and no man can attain the depth of His ways — so He said here as well, \"I will be with whoever is,\" by My name that you shall tell them, which is <b>Ehyeh</b>, for through it I am with man to grant grace and mercy.\n\nThe Gaon Rav Saadia wrote that its meaning is: who has not passed and will not pass, for He is first and He is last. His words are close to the words of Rabbi Isaac. And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (I:63): \"the existent that exists\" — that is, the worthy of existence. He meant to explain to them that there is an existent worthy of existence that was never absent and will never be absent.\n\n<b>Now</b>, according to the opinion of these scholars, we must explain that HaShem said to Moses that he should say this name to them and teach it to them — that is, that he should set forth for them rational proofs through which this name is established among their wise men: namely, existence or permanence that has not passed and will not pass. For mere mention of the name, whatever one may say, is not a proof to remove from them the doctrine of eternity [of the universe] or the heresy that is reckoned as the absolute negation of existence. And this is not the meaning of the verse. Rather, the mention of the name to them is the proof, and it is the sign and the wonder concerning the matter they asked of him.\n\nIn my opinion, the elders of Israel would not have doubted the existence of the Creator, as the Rav said — heaven forbid. Rather, this name is the answer to his question that we have explained to you: He informed him that he is sent to them in the attribute of justice that is within the attribute of mercy, and He said, <b>Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: Ehyeh has sent me to you</b> — that he should mention to them the one name alone, to indicate unity. And therefore He further commanded him, <b>Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: Ehyeh has sent me</b> (Exodus 3:15), for this name is the attribute of mercy, and they will know that He will lead at the right hand of Moses, the arm of His splendor (Isaiah 63:12), and that He will renew signs and wonders in the world.\n\n<b>Now</b>, He explained to him that Ehyeh, which He commanded him to say to them, is this great Name, and they are equivalent in their expression and in their letters. For the two final letters of the first name are the ones that precede in this one: for in the first they indicate the wisdom of Solomon, as it is written, \"And YHWH gave wisdom to Solomon\" (1 Kings 5:26), and in this one they indicate the wisdom of Elohim. And the letter א in the first indicates eternity and oneness, and the letter י in the second indicates ten sefirot without substance. And these two letters, א and י, were taken from the two holy names and written at the beginning and end in the name that is written and read with א-ד (Adonai), to indicate that it is emanation from the head to the end, with the attribute of judgment in the middle, as it is written, \"for My name is in him\" (Exodus 23:21). And from here you will understand why it is contracted. And the Holy Tongue makes use of that name with every expression of Adonai, for from it comes all dominion and authority. And may HaShem show us wonders from His Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase 'יורה הוראה שלמה' appears to be a transcription error; I have read it as belonging to comment 1 based on the source text, where Moses asks that God reveal a name indicating 'complete proof/guidance.' The reading here reflects the Chavel edition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:03:21.007713635Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}